{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The most probable outcome of Lottery A is 3000 Euros with a probability of 90%, which is the highest probability in that lottery. The most probable outcome of Lottery B is 6000 Euros with 45% probability, which is the highest probability in that lottery (though less than 50%). The decision reason compares these most probable outcomes: 3000 Euros at 90% vs. 6000 Euros at 45%. Since 3000 Euros is less favorable than 6000 Euros in monetary terms, but 90% is a higher probability than 45%, the reason evaluates which lottery has the more favorable outcome among the most probable ones. Here, 'more favorable' would typically mean higher monetary value, so the more favorable most probable outcome is 6000 Euros (from B), even though it occurs with lower probability. Thus, the decision reason would prefer Lottery B over A, because its most probable outcome (6000€) is more favorable than that of A (3000€), despite the lower probability.",
