  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on identifying the most probable outcome in each lottery and then comparing the monetary favorability of those outcomes. It would favor the lottery whose most probable outcome has the higher monetary value (here, B with 6000€ at 45%). However, the individual does not mention or compare the outcomes (3000€ vs. 6000€) at all. Instead, the individual evaluates the attractiveness of the probability structure, preferring A because its high probability (90%) stands out more clearly than B’s 45%. This suggests a focus on probability salience or certainty, not on the most probable outcome’s value. There is no evidence that the individual identified the most probable outcomes or compared them based on monetary favorability. Therefore, the information considered (probability attractiveness, clarity) differs from that required by the reason (most probable outcome and its value), and the justification (preference for high, clear probabilities) does not align with the reason’s rule (prefer the lottery with the more favorable most probable outcome).",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

